```

### Data Cleaning

RNA-Seq counts per million data was acquired from Stemformatics
[@stemform], using the Carcamo-Orive (2017) dataset
[@carcamo-orive2017]. Genes in this study were referred to by their
Ensembl ID, so gene information was integrated from Emsembl database
[@ensembl]. To begin to clean the data, the Ensembl gene ID in both the
count per million data frame and Ensembl data frame were mutated, using
the dplyr package [@R-dplyr] from tidyverse [@R-tidyverse], to the name:
Gene stable ID. The Ensembl gene data frame was then filtered for the
genes in the count per million data set (reducing the number of genes
from 86,369 to 56,693).

*further cleaning of the count per million data will be added*

### Figure 1

A new column was then added to the Ensembl data frame called Category,
which uses the Gene type column to categorize the genes into the
following categories: Protein coding, long non coding RNA (lncRNA),
Small RNAs, Pseudogenes, and Other. A count of the number of genes in
each category was then performed by using group by Category and
summarizing a count. A fraction and percentage were then calculated by
adding a column to the data frame that divides the count by the sum of
count (i.e. total count for all categories). This summarized data was
later plotted as a pie chart, so modifications were made to the
summarized data, such as defining ymax, ymin, and label position, for
ease of plotting. Text labeled were also created, which include the
category and the percentage calculated above. Percentages over 10% where
then added to a subcategory, called large slice, so that large
categories could be labeled on the slices but small categories would be
labeled outside of the pie chart. The scales [@R-scales] package from
ggplot2 [@R-ggplot2] was used to create the pie chart, by plotting "" on
the x axis, fraction on the y axis, and fill by category. Geom_bar and
coord_polar were used to format the pie chart, using fraction for the
size and angle of the slices. Theme_void was then used to remove the
default plot elements, creating a white background. Plot.margin was then
set to 0 on all sides to remove white space around the plot. Labels on
large slices were added by using the subset, large slice categories, and
adding a label using the label position and label text defined above.
Repel labels were added to categories not in large slices, using the
same label position and label text.

*continue methods for following figures ...*

```{r, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#library packages
library(tidyverse)
library(dplyr)
library(scales)
library(ggrepel)
library(pheatmap)
library(lme4)
library(lmerTest)  
library(emmeans)
library(broom)
library(ggplot2)
library(ggsignif)
library(ggsci)

#import data files
raw_counts <- read_tsv("stemformatics_dataset_7274.samples.tsv")
cpm_data   <- read_tsv("stemformatics_dataset_7274.cpm.tsv")
ensembl_data <- read_csv("mart_export.txt")

# view data files
#head(raw_counts)
#head(cpm_data)
```

```{r, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
#Change column name from "0.001" to "Gene Stable ID"
cpm_data <- cpm_data %>%
  rename("Gene stable ID" = "...1")

#Create a data frame with only the gene info for the genes in this study
ensembl_filtered <- ensembl_data %>%
  filter(`Gene stable ID` %in% cpm_data$`Gene stable ID`)

```

```{r, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
# Add a new column 'Category' based on 'Gene type'
ensembl_filtered <- ensembl_filtered %>%
  mutate(Category = case_when(
    `Gene type` %in% c("protein_coding") ~ "Protein coding",
    `Gene type` %in% c("lncRNA", "TEC") ~ "lncRNA",
    `Gene type` %in% c("miRNA", 
                          "snoRNA", 
                          "snRNA", 
                          "scaRNA", 
                          "sRNA", 
                          "vault_RNA", 
                          "ribozyme") ~ "Small RNAs",
    `Gene type` %in% c("pseudogene", 
                          "processed_pseudogene", 
                          "unprocessed_pseudogene",
                          "transcribed_unprocessed_pseudogene",
                          "transcribed_processed_pseudogene",
                          "unitary_pseudogene", 
                          "IG_C_pseudogene", 
                          "IG_V_pseudogene",
                          "IG_J_pseudogene", 
                          "TR_J_pseudogene", 
                          "TR_V_pseudogene",
                          "translated_processed_pseudogene",
                        "transcribed_unitary_pseudogene") ~ "Pseudogenes",
       `Gene type` %in% c("misc_RNA", 
                          "artifact", 
                          "Mt_tRNA", 
                          "IG_V_gene", 
                          "IG_J_gene", 
                          "IG_C_gene", 
                          "IG_D_gene",
                          "TR_J_gene", 
                          "TR_V_gene", 
                          "TR_D_gene", 
                          "TR_C_gene",
                          "rRNA", 
                          "Mt_rRNA", 
                          "rRNA_pseudogene") ~ "Other",
   TRUE ~ "Other"))

#summarize count of genes by RNA catagory
RNA_types_summary <- ensembl_filtered %>%
  group_by(Category) %>%
  summarize(count = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
  mutate(fraction = count / sum(count),
         ymax = cumsum(fraction),
         ymin = c(0, head(ymax, n=-1)),
         label_pos = (ymax + ymin)/2,
         label_text = paste0(Category, "\n", scales::percent(fraction)),
         large_slice = fraction > 0.1)
```

# Results

```{r fig-rna-type, fig.cap = "Detected RNA Types. The number of genes detected in each RNA category from RNA-seq data were counted and plotted as a pie chart. Small categories (< 2 %), such as rRNA, tRNA, and immunoglobulin genes, were combined into an 'Other' category.", fig.width=8, fig.height=8, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}

# Pie chart of number of genes per RNA catagory
ggplot(RNA_types_summary, aes(x = "", y = fraction, fill = Category)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity", width = 1, color = "white") +
  coord_polar(theta = "y") +
  theme_void() +
theme(
  legend.position = "none",
  plot.margin = margin(0, 0, 0, 0)) +
  # On-slice labels for large slices
  geom_text(
    data = subset(RNA_types_summary, large_slice),
    aes(y = label_pos, label = label_text),
    size = 7,
    fontface = "bold",
    color = "white") +
  # Repelled labels for small slices
 geom_text_repel(
    data = subset(RNA_types_summary, !large_slice),
    aes(x = 1, y = label_pos, label = label_text),  
    size = 7,
    fontface = "bold",
    nudge_x = 0.8,
    force = 0.5,
    segment.color = "grey60",
    segment.length = 0.2,
    show.legend = FALSE) +
  labs(fill = "Category")
```

Figure 2. Expression by RNA Type (similar to figure 1 but expression
based rather than count based)

```{r fig-rna-expression, fig.cap = "Expression by RNA Type.", fig.cap = "Expression by RNA Types. The expression of genes, using raw count per million data, was averaged by type of RNA and plotted as a pie chart. Small categories (< 2 %), such as rRNA, tRNA, and immunoglobulin genes, were combined into an 'Other' category.",fig.width=8, fig.height=8, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}

# pivot raw cpm data and add ensembl data information by Gene. then remove NAs
cpm_long <- cpm_data %>%
  pivot_longer(-`Gene stable ID`, names_to = "Sample", values_to = "Expression") %>%
  left_join(ensembl_filtered, by = "Gene stable ID") %>%
  filter(!is.na(Category), !is.na(Expression)) 

# summarize expression per category 
cpm_category_summary <- cpm_long %>%
  group_by(Category) %>%
  summarize(Total_Expression = sum(Expression)) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  mutate(
    fraction = Total_Expression / sum(Total_Expression),
    label_text = paste0(Category, "\n", round(fraction * 100, 1), "%"),
    label_pos = cumsum(fraction) - fraction / 2,
    large_slice = fraction > 0.1)  

#create a pie chart
ggplot(cpm_category_summary, aes(x = "", y = fraction, fill = Category)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity", width = 1, color = "white") +
  coord_polar(theta = "y") +
  theme_void() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "none",
    plot.margin = margin(0, 0, 0, 0)) +
  # On-slice labels for large slices
  geom_text(
    data = subset(cpm_category_summary, large_slice),
    aes(y = label_pos, label = label_text),
    size = 7,
    fontface = "bold",
    color = "white") +
  # Repelled labels for small slices
  geom_text_repel(
    data = subset(cpm_category_summary, !large_slice),
    aes(x = 1, y = label_pos, label = label_text),
    size = 7,
    fontface = "bold",
    nudge_x = 0.8,
    force = 0.5,
    segment.color = "grey60",
    segment.length = 0.2,
    show.legend = FALSE) +
  labs(fill = "Category")
```

```{r, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#begin cleaning data 

#verify that the sample ids match in raw_counts and _cpm_data
raw_counts <- raw_counts[match(colnames(cpm_data)[-1], raw_counts$sample_id), ]

all(colnames(cpm_data)[-1] == raw_counts$sample_id)

#convert counts per million to a log scale, excluding the first column which has gene info, and adding an offset so that values use to 0 are not skewed
logCPM <- cbind(`Gene stable ID` = cpm_data$`Gene stable ID`, log2(cpm_data[,-1] + 0.5))

#summary(raw_counts)
#unique(raw_counts$parental_cell_type) #all parent cells are blood mononuclear
#unique(raw_counts$disease_state) #all normal
#unique(raw_counts$sex) #male and female
#unique(raw_counts$developmental_stage) #33 ages 

#based on information regarding the samples, sex and age differ across the samples.

#age is reported as "age years old", so a new column was added with just the numeric
raw_counts$age_numeric <- as.numeric(sub("-year-old.*", "", raw_counts$developmental_stage))

#age was then grouped by decade to reduce the number of conditions 
raw_counts$age_decade <- cut(
  raw_counts$age_numeric,
  breaks = seq(40, 80, by = 10),
  labels = c("40s", "50s", "60s", "70s"),
  right = FALSE)

# add gene name from ensembl filtered to longCPM data
logCPM_named <- logCPM %>%
  left_join(
    ensembl_filtered %>% select(`Gene stable ID`, `Gene name`),
    by = c("Gene stable ID" = "Gene stable ID"))

# move gene name to first column
logCPM_named <- logCPM_named %>%
  select(`Gene name`, everything())
```

Figure 3. Expression of Pluripotency Genes (SOX2, POU5F1(OCT4), KLF4, MYC (c-Myc),
TRA-1-81 and 1-60 (PODXL), NANOG, FUT4 (SSEA4), LIN28A, LIN28B, ESRRB, PRDM14, ZRP42 (REX1), TFCP2L1

(ectoderm: PAX6, NESTIN or
SOX1 (neural projenitor/stem cell markers), KRT14 (epithelial),
endoderm: SOX17, AFP (hepatic), PDX1 (pancreatic), mesoderm: CD34
(hematopoitic), CD90 (mesenchymal), NKX2.5 (cardiac progenitor))

```{r}
#create a list of pluripotency, differentiation, and housekeeping genes
pluripotency_genes <- c("SOX2", "POU5F1", "KLF4", "MYC",
                        "PODXL", "NANOG", "FUT4", "LIN28A", 
                        "LIN28B", "ESRRB", "PRDM14", "TFCP2L1")

differentiation_genes <- c("SOX1","PAX6","NESTIN","KRT14", 
                           "SOX17", "AFP", "PDX1", "CD34", 
                           "CD90", "NKX2.5")

housekeeping_genes <- c("ACTB","GAPDH","RPL13A","HPRT1",
                        "B2M","TBP","PGK1","UBC", "RPL36AL")
```

```{r, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
#utilize a function to generate heatmap, lmm, and bar graph with ANOVA and Tukey HSD

run_gene_analysis <- function(gene_list, gene_set_name) {
  
  # Filter logCPM to the selected genes and pivot long
  df <- logCPM_named %>%
    select(-`Gene stable ID`) %>%
    filter(`Gene name` %in% gene_list) %>%
    pivot_longer(-`Gene name`, names_to = "Sample", 
                 values_to = "Expression") %>%
    left_join(
      raw_counts %>% select(sample_id, sex, age_numeric, age_decade),
      by = c("Sample" = "sample_id")) %>%
    mutate(age_sex = paste(age_decade, sex, sep = " ")) %>%
    filter(!is.na(age_decade) & !is.na(sex))
  
  # Mixed model
  lmm <- lmer(Expression ~ sex + age_decade + (1|`Gene name`), data = df)
  
  # create empty dataframes to store results
  gene_anova <- data.frame()
  gene_tukey <- data.frame()
  
#use a for loop to loop through each gene and perform an ANOVa and tukey tests
  for (g in unique(df$`Gene name`)) {
    gdat <- df %>% filter(`Gene name` == g)
    # One-way ANOVA (Expression ~ sex_age)
    fit <- aov(Expression ~ age_sex, data = gdat)
    
    # Save tidy ANOVA table
    gene_anova <- rbind(
      gene_anova,
      broom::tidy(fit) %>% mutate(Gene = g))
    
    # Tukey HSD post-hoc for age_sex
    tk <- TukeyHSD(fit, "age_sex")
    tkdf <- as.data.frame(tk$age_sex) %>%
      rename(p_value = `p adj`) %>%
      mutate(Gene = g, comparison = rownames(.))
    
    gene_tukey <- rbind(gene_tukey, tkdf)
    
  # FDR correction for ANOVA
anova_fdr <- gene_anova %>%
  group_by(term) %>%
  mutate(p_adj = p.adjust(p.value, method = "BH")) %>%
  ungroup()

# FDR correction for Tukey HSD
tukey_fdr <- gene_tukey %>%
  group_by(Gene) %>%
  mutate(p_adj = p.adjust(p_value, method = "BH"))
  }
  
  # Summaries for plotting
  summary_df <- df %>%
    group_by(`Gene name`, age_sex) %>%
    summarize(
      mean_expr = mean(Expression),
      SE_expr   = sd(Expression)/sqrt(n()),
      .groups = "drop"
    ) %>%
    rename(Gene = `Gene name`, `Sample name` = age_sex)
  
  # Significant pairs
  sig_pairs <- gene_tukey %>%
    filter(p_value < 0.05) %>%
    mutate(
      annotations = case_when(
        p_value < 0.001 ~ "***",
        p_value < 0.01  ~ "**",
        TRUE ~ "*"
      ),
      group1 = sub("-.*", "", comparison),
      group2 = sub(".*-", "", comparison)
    ) %>%
    select(Gene, group1, group2, p_value, annotations)
  
  if (nrow(sig_pairs) > 0) {
    sig_pairs <- sig_pairs %>%
      left_join(
        summary_df %>%
          group_by(Gene) %>%
          summarize(max_bar = max(mean_expr + SE_expr)),
        by = "Gene"
      ) %>%
      group_by(Gene) %>%
      mutate(y_position = max_bar * (1 + 0.3 * row_number())) %>%
      ungroup()
  }
  
  # Bar graph
  plot <- ggplot(summary_df, aes(x = `Sample name`, y = mean_expr, fill = `Sample name`)) +
    geom_col() +
    geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = mean_expr - SE_expr, ymax = mean_expr + SE_expr),
                  width = 0.2) +
    facet_wrap(~ Gene, scales = "free_y") +
    scale_fill_d3("category20c") +
    theme_bw() +
    theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
          legend.position = "top",
          strip.text = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold")) +
    labs(title = paste(gene_set_name, "Gene Expression"),
         y = "Log2 CPM")
  
  if (nrow(sig_pairs) > 0) {
    plot <- plot +
      geom_signif(
        data = sig_pairs,
        aes(xmin = group1, xmax = group2, y_position = y_position,
            annotations = annotations),
        manual = TRUE,
        inherit.aes = FALSE
      )
  }

  heatmap_mat <- df %>%
  group_by(`Gene name`, age_sex) %>%
  summarize(Expression = mean(Expression, na.rm = TRUE),
            .groups = "drop") %>%
  pivot_wider(names_from = age_sex, values_from = Expression) %>%
  column_to_rownames("Gene name") %>%
  as.matrix()

heatmap_obj <- pheatmap::pheatmap(
  heatmap_mat,
  scale = "row",                 
  cluster_rows = TRUE,           
  cluster_cols = FALSE,          
  show_rownames = TRUE,
  show_colnames = TRUE,
  fontsize_row = 10,
  fontsize_col = 12,
  main = "Pluripotency Gene Expression by Age and Sex",
  cellwidth = 50,                
  cellheight = 25,
  silent = TRUE)

  # Return everything
  list(
  data   = df,
  lmm    = lmm,
  anova  = gene_anova,
  anova_fdr = anova_fdr,      
  tukey  = gene_tukey,
  tukey_fdr = tukey_fdr,      
  plot   = plot
  )
}

pluri_results  <- run_gene_analysis(pluripotency_genes, "Pluripotency")
diff_results   <- run_gene_analysis(differentiation_genes, "Differentiation")
house_results  <- run_gene_analysis(housekeeping_genes, "Housekeeping")
```


```{r fig-pluripotency, fig.cap = "Expression of Pluripotency Genes", fig.width=8, fig.height=8, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
pluri_results$heatmap
```

```{r fig-pluripotency2, fig.cap = "Expression of Pluripotency Genes", fig.width=10, fig.height=8, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
pluri_results$plot
```

```{r, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
#manual analysis before using function 

#create a data frame with just pluripotency genes, samples, and their log expression 
logCPM_pluripotency <- logCPM_named %>%
  select(-`Gene stable ID`) %>%
  filter(`Gene name` %in% pluripotency_genes) %>%
  pivot_longer(-`Gene name`, names_to = "Sample", values_to = "Expression")

#add sample info from raw_counts
logCPM_pluripotency <- logCPM_pluripotency %>%
  left_join(
    raw_counts %>% select(sample_id, sex, age_numeric, age_decade),
    by = c("Sample" = "sample_id"))
#create a column with both age and sex info
logCPM_pluripotency <- logCPM_pluripotency %>%
  mutate(age_sex = paste(age_decade, sex, sep = " ")) %>%
  filter(!is.na(age_decade) & !is.na(sex))

#check for missing genes 
missing_genes <- setdiff(pluripotency_genes, logCPM_pluripotency$`Gene name`)
missing_genes

#linear mixed model for pluripotency gene expression across iPSC samples

# Fit mixed model
#model the response variable, Expression, for the fixed effects, age and sex
# genes are treated as a random effect, all with different y intercepts, since expression levels vary by gene
lmm <- lmer(Expression ~ sex + age_decade + (1|`Gene name`), 
            data = logCPM_pluripotency)

summary(lmm)
#type III ANOVA to test significance of fixed effects on expression
anova(lmm)  

# estimated marginal means (or least-squares means) with pairwise comparisons
emmeans(lmm, pairwise ~ age_decade)
emmeans(lmm, pairwise ~ sex)
```

Fixed effects
Sex effect (male): Expression is slightly higher in males by 0.036 logCPM; significant at p = 0.0196.

Age effects: Only the 70s group is significantly higher than 40s (p = 0.0125). 50s and 60s are not significantly different from 40s.

Type III Analysis of Variance Table with Satterthwaite's method - confirms both sex and age_decade have a significant effect on pluripotency gene expression when averaged across all genes.

Predicted mean logCPM expression (adjusted means, not raw means)

age_decade emmean
 40s          6.20 
 50s          6.18 
 60s          6.21 
 70s          6.28 
 
Overall, pluripotency gene expression is slightly higher in males compared to females, regardless of age. Expression also shows an increasing trend with age, with samples from individuals in their 70s being significantly higher than those from individuals in their 40s. Differences between other age decades (50s, 60s) and 40s were not statistically significant.

```{r}
#ANOVA and Tukey HSD

#create empty dataframes to store results
gene_anova_results <- data.frame()
gene_tukey_results <- data.frame()

#use a for loop to loop through each gene and perform an ANOVa and tukey tests
for (g in unique(logCPM_pluripotency$`Gene name`)) {
  
  gene_data <- logCPM_pluripotency %>%
    filter(`Gene name` == g)
  
  # One-way ANOVA (Expression ~ sex_age)
  fit <- aov(Expression ~ age_sex, data = gene_data)
  
  # Save tidy ANOVA table
  anova_tbl <- broom::tidy(fit) %>%
    mutate(Gene = g)
  
  gene_anova_results <- rbind(gene_anova_results, anova_tbl)
  
  # Tukey HSD post-hoc for age_sex
  tukey <- TukeyHSD(fit, "age_sex")
  tukey_df <- as.data.frame(tukey$age_sex) %>%
    rename(p_value = `p adj`) %>% 
    mutate(Gene = g,
           comparison = rownames(.))
  
  gene_tukey_results <- rbind(gene_tukey_results, tukey_df)
}

# ANOVA FDR correction
pluripotency_gene_anova_results <- gene_anova_results %>%
  group_by(term) %>%
  mutate(p_adj = p.adjust(p.value, method = "BH")) %>%
  ungroup()

# Tukey HSD p-value adjustment
pluripotency_gene_tukey_results <- gene_tukey_results %>%
  group_by(Gene) %>%
  mutate(p_adj = p.adjust(p_value, method = "BH"))  

#see the number of p values less than 0.05
#sum(gene_tukey_results$p_value < 0.05, na.rm = TRUE)
```

```{r fig-bargraph-pluripotency, fig.cap = "Pluripotency Gene Expression by Age and Sex.", fig.width=8, fig.height=10, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
#graph Tukey HSD results using bar graph 

#summarize mean and SE
pluripotency_summary_data <- logCPM_pluripotency %>%
  group_by(`Gene name`, age_sex) %>%
  summarize(
    mean_expr = mean(Expression, na.rm = TRUE),
    SE_expr = sd(Expression, na.rm = TRUE)/sqrt(n()),
    .groups = "drop") %>%
  rename(Gene = `Gene name`, `Sample name` = age_sex)

# keep only significant pairs (adjusted p < 0.05)
pluripotency_all_sig_pairs <- gene_tukey_results %>%
  filter(p_value < 0.05) %>%
  mutate(
    annotations = case_when(
      p_value < 0.001 ~ "***",
      p_value < 0.01  ~ "**",
      p_value < 0.05  ~ "*",
      TRUE ~ ""),
    group1 = sub("-.*", "", comparison),  
    group2 = sub(".*-", "", comparison)) %>%
  select(Gene, group1, group2, p_value, annotations)

#calculate y position for significance asterisks 
if(nrow(pluripotency_all_sig_pairs) > 0){
  pluripotency_all_sig_pairs <- pluripotency_all_sig_pairs %>%
    left_join(
      pluripotency_summary_data %>%
        group_by(Gene) %>%
        summarize(max_bar = max(mean_expr + SE_expr, na.rm = TRUE)),
      by = "Gene") %>%
    group_by(Gene) %>%
    mutate(pair_index = row_number(),
           y_position = max_bar * (1 + 1.2 * pair_index)) %>%  
    ungroup()
}

pluripotency_bargraph <- ggplot(pluripotency_summary_data, aes(x = `Sample name`, y = mean_expr, fill = `Sample name`)) +
  geom_col(position = position_dodge(width = 0.9)) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = mean_expr - SE_expr, ymax = mean_expr + SE_expr),
                position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), width = 0.2) +
  facet_wrap(~ Gene, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0.02, 0.2))) +
  theme_bw() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
        legend.position = "top",
        panel.spacing = unit(1.2, "lines"),
        panel.clip = "off",
        axis.text.y = element_text(size = 12),
        axis.title.x = element_text(size = 16),
        axis.title.y = element_text(size = 16),
        strip.text = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold")) +
  labs(y = "Log2(Counts Per Million)",
       x = "Sample",
       fill ="Sample:") +
  scale_fill_d3("category20c")

if(nrow(pluripotency_all_sig_pairs) > 0){
  pluripotency_bargraph <- pluripotency_bargraph + geom_signif(data = pluripotency_all_sig_pairs, 
      aes(xmin = group1, xmax = group2, y_position = y_position,
      annotations = annotations),
      manual = TRUE, inherit.aes = FALSE, color = "black", textsize = 5)
}

pluripotency_bargraph
```


Figure 4. Expression of Differentiation Genes (ectoderm: PAX6, NESTIN or
SOX1 (neural projenitor/stem cell markers), KRT14 (epithelial),
endoderm: SOX17, AFP (hepatic), PDX1 (pancreatic), mesoderm: CD34
(hematopoitic), CD90 (mesenchymal), NKX2.5 (cardiac progenitor))

Figure 5. Expression of Housekeeping Genes (some sort of general figure
or sorted by housekeeping type (i.e. ribosomal, metabolic, cytoskeleton,
etc.))

Figure 6. Expression of Housekeeping Genes (focusing on some genes,
either over time during reprogramming or across different conditions)

these figures may be split into more than the six described depending on
the data

figures will include heat maps, bar charts, and possibly volcano plots


